<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  DeviceMatchingInfo.plist — what the Image Capture discovery daemon (icdd)
  reads out of a module's Contents/Resources/ to decide which Bonjour service
  types this module drives. icdd warns "Missing DeviceMatchingInfo.plist in
  '<bundle>'!" when a module in the Devices directory lacks this file.

  SCHEMA (corrected in Plan 2 Task 1b). The load spike's first reconstruction
  used ICABonjourServiceTypeKey / bonjour / serviceType / devices / deviceType,
  guessed from icdd's symbols. That was wrong — icdd read the file without the
  "Missing…" warning but instantiated no device. Inspecting the one shipping
  example, Apple's /System/Library/Image Capture/Devices/AirScanScanner.app,
  shows the real shape:

      BonjourNetwork = {
          "<_service._tcp.>" = ( { "device type" = scanner; } );
          ...
      };
      Version = "1.0";

  i.e. a top-level "BonjourNetwork" dict keyed by the Bonjour service type,
  each value an array of match dicts carrying the key "device type" (with the
  space) = "scanner", plus a top-level "Version" string.

  AirScanScanner matches the eSCL service types _uscan._tcp. / _uscans._tcp.
  We drive the Brother raw-scan protocol advertised on _scanner._tcp., so we
  key the same structure on that service type instead of cloning Apple's.

  MATCH-BIND FIX (Plan 2 Task 1d). The Task 1b/1c bundle declared this service
  type with a bare match dict — { "device type" = scanner; } and nothing else —
  and icdd browsed _scanner._tcp., saw the device, but never bound it to this
  module (Image Capture DEVICES = 0; our os_log subsystem stayed silent, i.e.
  no launch was even attempted — the match/binding gap, not a signing gate).

  Task 1d found the missing criterion. icdd binds a browsed Bonjour device to a
  module's match dict via its own selector
  compareBonjourDeviceModuleDictionary:withBonjourTXTRecord: (a symbol in
  /System/Library/Image Capture/Support/icdd). That comparison needs an
  ICABonjourTXTRecordKey sub-dict of TXT key/value pairs to test against the
  device's advertised TXT record; a match dict carrying only "device type"
  gives it nothing to compare and does not bind. ICABonjourTXTRecordKey is
  Apple's public match interface: AirScanScanner.app -- Apple's own module in
  /System/Library/Image Capture/Devices -- uses exactly this key to match its
  service type against Bonjour TXT pairs. We apply the same public interface to
  _scanner._tcp., keying on the TXT pairs this device broadcasts (below) plus
  "device events" = ( scan ). No vendor file, source, or proprietary content is
  consulted or copied.

  The two TXT values below are the device's OWN publicly-broadcast Bonjour TXT
  record, captured independently with
    dns-sd -L "Brother MFC-J6920DW" _scanner._tcp local
  which returns, on port 54921 (our raw-scan protocol):
    ... ty=Brother MFC-J6920DW mfg=Brother mdl=MFC-J6920DW button=T feeder=T
        flatbed=T ...
  So mfg=Brother and mdl=MFC-J6920DW are the exact strings icdd will compare.
  The model name is the project's public target device (repo name, docs/), not
  a per-unit secret; the synthetic per-unit Bonjour NAME BRW00AABBCCDDEE (a
  MAC-derived .local host) stays in DeviceInfo.plist and is never used here.

  This is reading a public plugin interface (the plist schema), not vendor
  source; the mdl/mfg strings are the device's own advertised TXT values.
  Device identity is the synthetic Bonjour name BRW00AABBCCDDEE (see
  DeviceInfo.plist).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>BonjourNetwork</key>
  <dict>
    <key>_scanner._tcp.</key>
    <array>
      <dict>
        <key>ICABonjourTXTRecordKey</key>
        <dict>
          <key>mdl</key>
          <string>MFC-J6920DW</string>
          <key>mfg</key>
          <string>Brother</string>
        </dict>
        <key>device events</key>
        <array>
          <string>scan</string>
        </array>
        <key>device type</key>
        <string>scanner</string>
      </dict>
    </array>
  </dict>
  <key>Version</key>
  <string>1.0</string>
</dict>
</plist>
